::SPEECH_U02_L01_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which speech focus is the clearest for a short presentation?
{
~A. I will talk about school.#This is too broad. A clear speech focus names the specific idea the audience should understand.
~B. School has many different topics.#This is not a focused speaking purpose. It gives a general category but no clear direction.
=C. Joining a school club can help students make friends and build confidence.#Correct. This gives one clear idea that can guide the main points of a speech.
~D. Clubs, lunch, homework, and sports are all important.#This combines unrelated ideas. A coherent speech needs one focus that all points support.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
A student is giving a speech about how to prepare for a class presentation. Which order is most logical?
{
~A. Practice delivery, choose a topic, create an outline.#This starts near the end of the process. The audience needs the preparation steps in a usable order.
~B. Create an outline, practice delivery, choose a topic.#This puts outlining before choosing the subject. The topic should come first.
~C. Practice delivery, create an outline, choose a topic.#This reverses the preparation process. Practice is more effective after the topic and outline are ready.
=D. Choose a topic, create an outline, practice delivery.#Correct. This order follows the natural process a speaker would use to prepare.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_GuidedPractice_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.PRES.02
Question:
Which part of a speech plan best shows that the speaker is following an accepted presentation format?
{
=A. Introduction, main points, transitions, and conclusion are clearly labeled.#Correct. These parts show a recognizable speech structure that supports quality presentation.
~B. The speaker writes every sentence in one long paragraph.#This may hide the structure. A speech format should make the parts easy to see and follow.
~C. The speaker lists random facts without grouping them.#Random facts do not create an organized presentation format. Ideas need structure.
~D. The speaker begins with the conclusion and never previews the topic.#This can confuse the audience. Accepted formats usually introduce the focus before developing it.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_GuidedPractice_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which transition best connects two points in an organized speech?
{
~A. Anyway, here is something else.#This sounds casual and does not explain the connection between ideas.
=B. After understanding the first benefit, we can also see how this helps students build skills.#Correct. This transition links the previous idea to the next idea and keeps the audience oriented.
~C. I do not know what to say next.#This weakens delivery and does not guide the audience.
~D. This has nothing to do with my first point.#This tells the audience the speech is losing focus instead of showing a connection.
}
::SPEECH_U02_L01_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U02_L01_GuidedPractice_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which conclusion best supports coherent focus?
{
~A. That is all I found, and there are many other things too.#This ending is vague. A conclusion should bring the audience back to the central idea.
~B. I also forgot to talk about several unrelated topics.#This introduces new confusion instead of closing the speech.
=C. School clubs matter because they help students connect, learn, and feel included.#Correct. This conclusion restates the main idea and summarizes the supporting points.
~D. Lunch schedules should probably change next year.#This shifts to a different topic and breaks the speech focus.
}
